  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 328 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nTroughing continues to develop across the Pacific Northwest this \nevening and overnight, along with a strengthening ridge across the \neastern CONUS. As we head into Saturday and Sunday, this pattern \namplifies, bringing continued strong warm air advection into the \narea tonight into Saturday. Temperatures could approach records \nacross the southern Red River Valley, with afternoon highs in the \nmid to upper 80s. Further north, widespread 70s to low 80s are \nlikely. An H5 trough will shift east late Saturday into Sunday \nmorning, ushering a cold front into the area. Rain chances are in \nthe forecast Sunday into Monday as this front traverses the region, \nwhich will accompany much cooler temperatures. Highs on Sunday are \nmost likely to occur at midnight for areas in eastern North Dakota \nand northwest Minnesota, with temperatures falling as the day \nprogresses. Locations in west central Minnesota should see another \nrelatively warm day before the front pushes through heading into \nSunday evening. \n\nRain chances will increase once more heading into mid week as \nanother system brings moisture into the Northern Plains and Upper \nMidwest. Ensemble spread is still somewhat large at this time, with \na wide range of potential QPF Wednesday through Friday. There is, \nhowever, relatively good agreement that most of the area will see \nmeasurable precipitation.",
